'''Ellen Foley''' (born June 5, 1951<ref>{{Cite news|title=This Day in St. Louis' 250th Anniversary|author=|date=June 5, 2014|work=St. Louis Post-Dispatch|page=A2|quote=Birthdays: Ellen Foley, singer and actress – 1951; Mary Engelbreit – 1952; Torry Holt, Rams wide receiver – 1976.|id={{ProQuest|1991436980}}}}</ref>) is an American singer and actress who has appeared on [[Broadway theatre|Broadway]] and television, where she co-starred in the hit [[NBC]] sitcom ''[[Night Court]]'' during its second season. In music, she has released five solo albums, but she is best known for her collaborations with rock singer [[Meat Loaf]], particularly the [[RIAA certification|14× Platinum]] selling 1977 album ''[[Bat Out of Hell]]''.<ref name="RIAA Database, Bat Out of Hell">{{cite web |title=RIAA Database, Bat Out of Hell|publisher = [[Recording Industry Association of America]]|url=https://www.riaa.com/goldandplatinumdata.php?tesultpage=1&table=SEARCH_RESULTS&action=&title=&artist=Meatloaf&format=&debutLP=&category=&sex=&releaseDate=&requestNo=&type=&level=&label=&company=&certificationDate=&awardDescription=&catalogNo=&aSex=&rec_id=&charField=&gold=&platinum=&multiPlat=&level2=&certDate=&album=&id=&after=&before=&startMonth=1&endMonth=1&startYear=1958&endYear=2011&sort=Artist&perPage=25 |access-date=October 22, 2023}}</ref><ref name="allamusic">{{cite web |url = {{AllMusic|class=artist|id=p17606|pure_url=yes}}|title = Ellen Foley > Overview |access-date = February 27, 2010|last = Ruhlmann|first = William |publisher= AllMusic.com}}</ref><ref name=sor_staff>{{cite web |url= http://www.schoolofrock.com/newyork/staff_school.php |title= School Staff |access-date=February 27, 2010|publisher= School of Rock (company) |url-status=dead |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20100329050659/http://www.schoolofrock.com/newyork/staff_school.php |archive-date=March 29, 2010 }}</ref>

== Early life and education ==
Foley was born in St. Louis, Missouri, the daughter of John and Virginia B. Foley. She attended [[Webster University]].<ref name="nytimes.com">{{Cite news|title = Douglas Bernstein Weds Ellen Foley, Fellow Actor|url = https://www.nytimes.com/1990/04/30/style/douglas-bernstein-weds-ellen-foley-fellow-actor.html|newspaper = The New York Times|date = 1990-04-30|access-date = 2015-12-02|issn = 0362-4331|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250119173611/https://www.nytimes.com/1990/04/30/style/douglas-bernstein-weds-ellen-foley-fellow-actor.html |archive-date=January 19, 2025}}</ref>

==Career==
===Vocalist===
Foley gained public recognition through singing a duet with [[Meat Loaf]] on the hit single "[[Paradise by the Dashboard Light]]" from the 1977 album ''[[Bat Out of Hell]]''.<ref name= tuc>{{cite journal |date=March 16, 1991 |title=The Uncut Crap - Over 56 Things You Never Knew About The Clash |journal=NME |volume=3 |publisher=IPC Magazines |location=London |issn=0028-6362 |oclc=4213418 |quote= 'Should I Stay or Should I Go' was written by Mick about American singer Ellen Foley, who sang the backing vocals on Meatloaf's Bat Out Of Hell LP }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://londonsburning.org/art_nme_03_16_91.html |title=The Uncut Crap - Over 56 Things You Never Knew About The Clash - NME March 16, 1991 |access-date=December 11, 2007 |last=Peterson |first=Tami |publisher=LondonsBurning.org |url-status= usurped |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20120717204427/http://londonsburning.org/art_nme_03_16_91.html |archive-date= July 17, 2012}}</ref> Foley's part was recorded individually and in one take with Meat Loaf present in the room so she could sing in character.<ref>{{Cite news|last=Gordon|first=Jeremy|date=2022-01-22|title=How Meat Loaf Made a Cult Favorite: 'Paradise by the Dashboard Light'|language=en-US|work=The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2022/01/22/arts/music/meat-loaf-paradise-by-the-dashboard-light.html|access-date=2022-01-23|issn=0362-4331}}</ref> Although [[Karla DeVito]] (who toured with Meat Loaf in support of the album) is featured in the music video, DeVito is lip synching to Foley's vocals.<ref>{{cite web |url= http://www.heyrick.co.uk/ricksworld/iacbtmn.html |access-date=February 27, 2010 |first=Richard |last=Murray| work=Rick's World |title=It's all coming back to me now | via= heyrick.co.uk }}</ref>

Foley's debut album ''[[Night Out (album)|Night Out]]'' was released in 1979; the album's single "What's a Matter Baby" reached No.&nbsp;7 in the Dutch charts and No.&nbsp;92 on the US charts.{{citation needed|date=September 2021}} The single "We Belong to the Night" reached No.1 in the Netherlands.<ref name="allmusic_no">{{cite web |url = {{AllMusic|class=album|id=r37890|pure_url=yes}}|title = Night Out > Overview |access-date = February 27, 2010|last = Stone|first = Doug| publisher=AllMusic.com}}</ref> The album, which peaked at No.&nbsp;152, was produced by [[Ian Hunter (singer)|Ian Hunter]] and [[Mick Ronson]]. Foley recorded a duet with Ian Hunter in 1980, [[Welcome to the Club (Ian Hunter album)|"We Gotta Get Outta Here"]]. Her creative relationship with Hunter led her to singing backing vocals on the [[Iron City Houserockers]]' 1980 album ''[[Have a Good Time but Get Out Alive!]]'', produced by Hunter, Ronson, and [[The E Street Band]]'s [[Steven Van Zandt]].<ref>{{cite web |url= http://www.ianhunter.com/bio.shtml |title= Biography | publisher= IanHunter.com| access-date=February 27, 2010 }}</ref>

Foley also sings in the title cut of the 1979 [[Blue Öyster Cult]] album ''[[Mirrors (Blue Öyster Cult album)|Mirrors]]'', and on [[The Clash]] album ''[[Sandinista!]]'' (released in 1980) in the songs "[[Hitsville UK]]" and "Corner Soul" (as well as the unreleased track "Blonde Rock 'n' Roll").{{citation needed|date=September 2021}} In 1981, all four members of The Clash appeared on her album ''[[Spirit of St. Louis (album)|The Spirit of St. Louis]]'', and [[Mick Jones (The Clash)|Mick Jones]] and [[Joe Strummer]] co-wrote a number of songs for the album.<ref name="allmusic_sosl">{{cite web |url = {{AllMusic|class=album|id=r37891|pure_url=yes}}|title = Spirit of St. Louis > Overview |access-date = May 28, 2009| publisher = AllMusic.com}}</ref> Jones produced the album, which featured members of [[The Blockheads]] and peaked at No.&nbsp;137 on the US charts.<ref name=allmusic_sosl /> In 1982, she provided backing vocals on The Clash's song "Car Jamming" from the album ''[[Combat Rock]]''.<ref>{{cite book| last= Gray| first= Marcus| title= The Clash: Return of the Last Gang in Town| page= 382| publisher= Hal Leonard Corporation| year= 2004 | isbn= 9780634082405}}</ref> The Clash's hit song "[[Should I Stay or Should I Go]]", written and sung by Jones, was about the turbulent relationship he shared with Foley at the time.<ref name=tuc /><ref>{{cite magazine |url=https://www.rollingstone.com/artists/theclash/biography |title=The Clash: Biography |access-date=February 27, 2010 |magazine= Rolling Stone |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090519093023/http://www.rollingstone.com/artists/theclash/biography |archive-date=May 19, 2009 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

She released her third solo album ''Another Breath'' in 1983; it failed to chart.<ref name="allmusic_ab">{{cite web |url = {{AllMusic|class=album|id=r37892|pure_url=yes}}|title = Another Breath > Overview |access-date = February 27, 2010| publisher=AllMusic.com}}</ref> In 1984, she sang backing vocals on [[Joe Jackson (musician)|Joe Jackson's]] album ''[[Body & Soul (Joe Jackson album)|Body & Soul]]'' and had a large role in the music video for [[Utopia (American band)|Utopia's]] "Crybaby".

Foley was one of four female vocalists to front the group [[Pandora's Box (band)|Pandora's Box]], formed by [[Jim Steinman]] in the late 1980s. Their album ''[[Original Sin (Pandora's Box album)|Original Sin]]'', released in 1989, was the first to feature the song "[[It's All Coming Back to Me Now]]" (vocals by Elaine Caswell); both [[Celine Dion]] and a duet between [[Meat Loaf]] and [[Marion Raven]] had separate chart successes with that song in some countries, years later.<ref>{{cite web |url= http://www.everyhit.co.uk/|title=UK Top 40 Chart Archive, British Singles & Album Charts|publisher=EveryHit.co.uk}}</ref><ref>{{Cite magazine|title = Celine Dion - Chart history |url = https://www.billboard.com/artist/298857/celine+dion/chart| magazine = Billboard|access-date = 2015-12-02}}</ref>

===Broadway, film and television===
Foley studied acting at [[HB Studio]]<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://hbstudio.org/about-hb-studio/alumni/|title=Notable Alumni|publisher=HB Studio}}</ref> in New York City. She has appeared on Broadway in such shows as ''[[Me and My Girl]]'' and the revival of ''[[Hair (musical)|Hair]]'', and off-Broadway in ''[[Beehive]]''. Foley originated the role of The Witch in [[Stephen Sondheim]]'s ''[[Into the Woods]]'' at the [[Old Globe Theater]] in San Diego <ref>{{cite web |author=<!-- not stated --> |date= |title='Into the Woods': World Premiere Production (1986)|url=https://ovrtur.com/production/2883281 |website=|location= |publisher=Ovrtur |access-date=3 October 2026}}</ref> but was replaced by [[Bernadette Peters]] before the musical opened on Broadway.<ref name=woods>[http://www.sondheimguide.com/woods.html#BWP "'Into the Woods', 1986 Old Globe Production and 1987 Broadway Production"], SondheimgGide.com, accessed August 2, 2012.</ref> Foley eventually played the role on Broadway from August 1, 1989 until the show's closing on September 3, 1989.<ref name=woods/>

Her best known television acting role is that of Billie Young on ''[[Night Court]]'' for season two (1984–85).{{Citation needed |date=February 2025}} She was succeeded by [[Markie Post]] as Christine Sullivan, who had been [[Reinhold Weege]]'s first choice for the public defender part. Post had been unavailable while under contract on the television series ''[[The Fall Guy]]'' on ABC.<ref name=Post>{{Cite journal|url=http://northhollywood.patch.com/articles/markie-post-interview-part-two|title=Markie Post Interview: Part 2|date=2011-01-20|access-date=2011-04-06|publisher=North Hollywood Toluca Lake Patch}}</ref> In mid-1985, it was publicly announced that Foley left ''Night Court'' "to pursue other acting and singing roles."<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.chicagotribune.com/1985/07/11/new-night-court-characters/ |title=New 'Night Court' characters |work=Chicago Tribune|date=11 July 1985 |access-date=23 October 2025}}</ref> However, Foley later said, “I was fired, man! Got bumped for Markie Post! Beats me, I don’t know why.”<ref>{{cite web|url=https://nymag.com/arts/popmusic/features/48672/ |title=The Ballad of Ellen Foley |work=New York|date=18 July 2008 |access-date=23 October 2025}}</ref> She also remarked, "You don’t know pain until you’ve been replaced in a role by Markie Post."<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.culturesonar.com/when-ellen-foley-met-mick-jones/ |title=When Ellen (Foley) Met Mick (Jones) |work=CultureSonar|date=9 April 2024 |access-date=22 October 2025}}</ref>  She had roles in [[Miloš Forman]]'s film adaptation of the stage musical ''[[Hair (film)|Hair]]'' (1979), as well as in the movies ''[[Fatal Attraction]]'' (1987), ''[[Married to the Mob]]'' (1988) and ''[[Cocktail (1988 film)|Cocktail]]'' (1988). Foley was in the short-lived 1977 series ''[[3 Girls 3]]'', co-starring with [[Debbie Allen]] and [[Mimi Kennedy]].

===Other pursuits===
In the mid-2000s, she taught voice at the [[School of Rock (company)|School of Rock]] (founded by [[Paul Green (musician)|Paul Green]]) in Manhattan.<ref name= sor_staff />

== Personal life ==
In 1990, Foley married writer Doug Bernstein, co-author of the Off-Broadway revue ''Showing Off''.<ref name="foleymarried">{{Cite web|date=April 30, 1990|title=Douglas Bernstein Weds Ellen Foley, Fellow Actor|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1990/04/30/style/douglas-bernstein-weds-ellen-foley-fellow-actor.html|work=The New York Times}}{{subscription required}}</ref>  They have two children, Henry and Timothy.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Ellen Foley - Latest News, Updates, Photos and Videos {{!}} Yahoo |url=https://www.yahoo.com/topics/ellen-foley/ |access-date=2025-04-02 |website=www.yahoo.com |language=en-US}}</ref> 

